From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 01:02:50 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Stop indexing promptly on shutdown

The block indexing loop ran to the end of its range whatever happened,
and shutdown closes the index, which waits for it. During a first index
or a long catch-up that is minutes to hours, so a service manager asking
Frigate to stop (an update, a restart) gave up and killed it. The loop
now checks the stop flag before each block and ends after the block in
hand; the next start resumes from the last block indexed.
---
 .../com/sparrowwallet/frigate/bitcoind/BitcoindClient.java  | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/main/java/com/sparrowwallet/frigate/bitcoind/BitcoindClient.java b/src/main/java/com/sparrowwallet/frigate/bitcoind/BitcoindClient.java
index b9fae88..2f03ecd 100644
--- a/src/main/java/com/sparrowwallet/frigate/bitcoind/BitcoindClient.java
+++ b/src/main/java/com/sparrowwallet/frigate/bitcoind/BitcoindClient.java
@@ -378,6 +378,12 @@ public class BitcoindClient {
         long lastLogTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
 
         for(int i = startHeight; i <= maxHeight; i++) {
+            //a stop during a long index (the first one, or a catch-up) ends it after the block in hand, rather than when the whole run
+            //is done: shutdown closes the index, which waits for this loop, and a service manager gives up waiting long before that
+            if(stopped) {
+                log.info("Stopping indexing at block {}", i);
+                return;
+            }
             try {
                 if(useGetBlockVerbose) {
                     indexBlockVerbose(i, hexFormat);
